      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright© Bentham Science Publishers; For any queries, please email at epub@benthamscience.org.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "AIM AND OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The aim of this study was to evaluate the association between folate level and the risk of schizophrenia and to identify possible biomarker for schizophrenia."
          },
          {
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Data about folate were extracted from 16 high quality studies. The association of folate level in blood and schizophrenia was evaluated using standardized mean difference (SMD) and 95% confidence interval (CI)."
          },
          {
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Totally 1183 (52.1%) cases and 1089 (47.9%) controls were included in the current metaanalysis. Folate level in schizophrenia patients was significantly lower than that in healthy controls (SMD= -0.65; 95% CI: [-0.86, -0.45]; P <0.00001). Subgroup analysis demonstrated that the decreased folate level was found in both Asian and European patients (SMD=-0.86, P<0.00001; SMD=-0.44, P<0.00001, respectively), while there were no significant differences in patients from other areas (P>0.05). Sensitivity analysis confirmed that these results were stable and reliable, no publication bias existed in our meta-analysis based on Egger's and Begg's tests (P=0.48 and 0.30, respectively)."
          },
          {
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "These results suggest that decreased folate may be a risk factor for schizophrenia. More epidemiological and biochemistry studies are required to describe how folate or folate supplementation play roles in the progress of schizophrenia."
          }
        ]
      },
